# What this framework may not name, anywhere under lib/, test/, bin/ and programs/.
#
# One word per line. A blank line and a line beginning with # are ignored, and everything else is
# matched case-insensitively and word-anchored: `snapshotOfState` is a captured state and not an
# occurrence of `snap`.
#
# WORD-ANCHORED CUTS BOTH WAYS, and the blocks below are written knowing it. Anything that is not a
# letter or a digit ends a word, so a bare name reports every longer one built on it with a
# separator: `letsencrypt` reports `letsencrypt-prod` and `LETSENCRYPT_EMAIL` alike. A longer name
# built WITHOUT a separator is a different word and has to stand in its own right — `digita` does
# not report `digitacloud`, and `simetrix` does not report `simetrixch`.
#
# THE LIST LIVES HERE, UNDER tool/, AND NOT BESIDE THE CHECK THAT READS IT. api-purity scans test/
# to the byte, so a list written into test/checks/api_purity_test.dart would itself be an occurrence
# of every word on it and the check could never pass. tool/ is the harness: the gate's own programs,
# none of it compiled into the framework or shipped with it, which is why the check may name what it
# forbids here. That tool/ stays out of the scan is asserted by a counter-probe of its own.
#
# Such knowledge does not arrive as a dependency, where a reviewer would meet it in the pubspec — it
# arrives one word at a time, in a doc comment that explains a port by the tool the author had in
# mind and in a test fixture that names a real thing because a real thing was handy. Both read as
# illustration on the day they are written and as specification a year later.

# The product built on this framework, the platform it stands on, and the organisation behind
# both. The same block stands on every plugin's list, word for word: a framework that may name no
# platform and a plugin that may name no application of its tool are the same scan pointed at two
# lists, and this is the part of the two lists that has to be identical.
hostyour
digita
digitacloud
digitaplatform
simetrix
simetrixch

# That platform's choices: the distribution it runs on, the components it deploys, the tools it
# drives those components with, the vendors it rents from, and the authority it gets its
# certificates from. A plugin leaves its OWN tool off its list, because knowing its tool is the
# whole point of it. The framework has no tool at all, so it carries every one of them.
microk8s
argocd
tekton
zot
authentik
external-secrets
vault
helm
kubectl
snap
netplan
cloudflare
hetzner
letsencrypt

# That platform's own domain words, and its own word for the branch every other branch is cut from.
tenant
consumer
trunk
